      slug: "mobile-first-product-design",
      title: "Mobile-First Product Design: Fewer Screens, Better Outcomes",
      excerpt:
        "Why we prototype on mobile viewports early—and how intentional UX constraints reduce rework and accelerate time-to-market.",
      content: `
      <h2>The Constraint That Unlocks Clarity</h2>
      <p>Designing for desktop screens first often tempts product teams into filling excess canvas space with non-essential widgets and complex navigational nests.</p>
      <p>Starting with mobile viewports forces ruthless prioritization: what is the single most valuable action a user must take on this view? Everything else becomes secondary.</p>

      <h3>Designing for the Thumb Zone</h3>
      <p>Key interactive controls, primary CTAs, and navigation triggers must sit within comfortable reach of one-handed thumb interaction. This subtle ergonomic decision drastically improves user retention and task completion speed.</p>
    `,
